  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 232 PM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nSynoptically, forecast confidence has increased for Sunday \nthrough around Tuesday night. On Sunday morning, an upper level \ntrough is expected to move eastward from the Western CO/WY \nborder and enter the Tri-State Border Area by the evening hours.\nAn associated weak jet streak should accompany this trough. By \nMonday evening, the trough exits to the east and our flow will \nmainly remain from the west-southwest until around Tuesday \nnight. \n\nHigh temperatures for the late weekend into the work week still\nlook to remain in the 70s for the majority of the period. \nExpect a brief warmup starting on Wednesday and Thursday with \nhighs in the lower 80s. Winds are once again calm to variable. \nSunday still looks to have the greatest chance of seeing \nprecipitation for the forecast. Both the LREF and NBM guidance \nshow at least around a 30-40% chance of exceeding 0.1 inches of \nprecip from the early morning and tapering off in the afternoon.\nMonday through Wednesday, the Probability of Precipitation is \naround 20-40% each afternoon for the majority of the CWA. \nGuidance indicates lower values of CAPE and moderate shear, \nwhich would lean more towards showers than severe storms. \n\nStarting Wednesday, the forecast becomes more uncertain. \nGuidance indicates that an upper level ridge should be set up \nover the Southern US/Gulf Region, alongside troughing over the \nWestern CONUS Tuesday night into Wednesday morning. However, \nWednesday morning is when the uncertainty begins. The GFS has an\nupper level ridge farther north over the AR/LA/TX border, \namplifying directly over the CWA. Both the ECMWF and the \nCanadian-NH models have the ridge centered over the TX/Gulf \nborder without amplifying overhead, placing it instead over CO \nand WY. Both the GFS and Canadian-NH models feature either a \nweak trough or a shortwave moving through the Tri-State Area. \nOne other feature to keep an eye on is, both the GFS and \nCanadian-NH model show a surface low developing out of \nSoutheastern CO. With that being said, where the ridge and \ntrough/shortwaves set up will either increase or decrease our \nprecipitation chances for Wednesday and Thursday. It will be \ninteresting to see how guidance handles this upper level pattern\nuncertainty as we get closer.",
